<td id="S1.T1.4.1.16.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">91.85</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.16.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">91.95</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.16.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">91.76</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.16.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">73.86</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.16.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">69.96</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.16.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">71.64</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.4.1.17" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.4.1.17.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.17.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.17.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">INSTRUCTOR-CIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite></span>
</span></th>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.17.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">88.83</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.17.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">88.81</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.17.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">88.77</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.17.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">84.24</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.17.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">82.52</td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.17.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">83.14</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.4.1.18" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0D2BE;">
<th id="S1.T1.4.1.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.18.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0D2BE;">
<span id="S1.T1.4.1.18.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">ABEX-RAT (Ours)</span>
</span></th>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.18.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.18.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0D2BE;">93.07</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.18.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.18.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0D2BE;">92.84</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.18.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.18.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0D2BE;">92.82</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.18.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.18.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0D2BE;">89.99</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.18.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.18.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0D2BE;">91.88</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.4.1.18.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.4.1.18.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F0D2BE;">90.32</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>METHODOLOGY</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the challenges of classifying occupational accident reports, particularly in scenarios with class imbalance, we propose the ABEX-RAT framework. This framework synergizes a novel data augmentation pipeline with a robust training strategy to enhance both model performance and efficiency. The name encapsulates its two core components: an abstractive-expansive (ABEX) data augmentation stage and a random adversarial training (RAT) method for classification. The overall architecture, illustrated in Figure 2, is a three-stage pipeline.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Stage 1: ABEX Data Augmentation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate data scarcity and class imbalance, we employ the ABEX generative data augmentation pipeline, which consists of two sequential steps. First, each raw accident report, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>raw</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is distilled into a concise abstract description, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{abs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>abs</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{abs}}</annotation></semantics></math>, using a LLM <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            https://www.modelscope.cn/models/Qwen/Qwen3-235B-A22B-Instruct-2507</span></span></span> guided by a prompt <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. This abstraction function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> retains the core, label-defining semantics:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{abs}}=\mathcal{A}_{\text{LLM},\mathcal{P}}(T_{\text{raw}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>abs</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>raw</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{abs}}=\mathcal{A}_{\text{LLM},\mathcal{P}}(T_{\text{raw}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Next, a pre-trained generative model <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            https://huggingface.co/utkarsh4430/ABEX-abstract-expand</span></span></span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> serves as an expansion function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate a set of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> diverse, full-text synthetic samples from each abstract. To address class imbalance, the number of generated samples, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, is set to be inversely proportional to the number of original samples in each class <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T_{\text{aug}}^{(1)},\dots,T_{\text{aug}}^{(R)}\}=\mathcal{E}_{\text{BART}}(T_{\text{abs}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext>aug</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext>aug</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>BART</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>abs</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{\text{aug}}^{(1)},\dots,T_{\text{aug}}^{(R)}\}=\mathcal{E}_{\text{BART}}(T_{\text{abs}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Stage 2: Feature Extraction</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following data augmentation, we adopt an efficient embedding-based paradigm. We use a powerful, off-the-shelf text embedding model (e.g., Qwen3-Embedding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>) as a fixed feature extractor <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Each text sample <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from the augmented dataset is mapped to a dense <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}=\Phi(T_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}=\Phi(T_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This process yields a dataset of embedding-label pairs, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{emb}}=\{(\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{emb}}=\{(\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i})\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which serves as the input for the final training stage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Stage 3: RAT Classification</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The final stage trains a lightweight multi-layer perceptron (MLP) classifier, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{x};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{x};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, on the semantic embeddings using our RAT mechanism. Adversarial training improves generalization by training on worst-case perturbations <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{\text{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{\text{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we approximate using the Fast Gradient Method (FGM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{\text{adv}}=\epsilon\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x};\theta),y)}{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x};\theta),y)\|_{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{\text{adv}}=\epsilon\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x};\theta),y)}{\|\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x};\theta),y)\|_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the loss function and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is the perturbation magnitude. To mitigate the computational expense, our RAT approach applies this perturbation stochastically. For each batch, a Bernoulli trial <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\sim\text{Bernoulli}(p_{\text{rat}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Bernoulli</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>rat</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\sim\text{Bernoulli}(p_{\text{rat}})</annotation></semantics></math> determines if the adversarial loss is added:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{batch}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{std}}+k\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{adv}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>batch</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>std</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{batch}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{std}}+k\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{std}}=\mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x};\theta),y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>std</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{std}}=\mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x};\theta),y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard loss on original embeddings, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{adv}}=\mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{r}_{\text{adv}};\theta),y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{adv}}=\mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{r}_{\text{adv}};\theta),y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the adversarial loss on perturbed embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further combat class imbalance, we instantiate the loss function <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> with the focal loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which focuses training on hard examples:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FL}(p_{t})=-\alpha_{t}(1-p_{t})^{\gamma}\log(p_{t})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>FL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FL}(p_{t})=-\alpha_{t}(1-p_{t})^{\gamma}\log(p_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability of the ground-truth class, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a focusing parameter, and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a class-balancing weight. By integrating focal loss into the RAT objective, our framework robustly handles both input perturbations and skewed data distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>EXPERIMENTS</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset and Metrics.</span> We evaluate our framework on the OSHA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, which contains 4770 construction accident reports across seven categories. We use a stratified 8:1:1 split for training, validation, and testing. Given the inherent class imbalance, we report both <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weighted-F1</span> for overall performance and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Macro-F1</span> to assess performance on infrequent classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span> Our PyTorch-based MLP classifier is trained for 100 epochs (batch size 16) using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. For our RAT mechanism, we set the Bernoulli probability <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{rat}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>rat</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{rat}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and the FGM perturbation magnitude <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. The focal loss is configured with a focusing parameter <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=3.0</annotation></semantics></math>. All experiments are conducted on a single NVIDIA 4090 GPU.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> We benchmark ABEX-RAT against three categories of models: (1) traditional fine-tuned methods (e.g., <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FastText</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BERT</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoBERTa</span>); (2) zero-shot large language models (e.g., <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.1-8B</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-8B</span>); and (3) domain-specific SOTA models, including a fine-tuned <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-8B<sub id="S3.SS1.p3.1.7.1" class="ltx_sub">SFT</sub></span> and the previous SOTA, <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">INSTRUCTOR-CIT</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Main Results and Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The primary experimental results are presented in Table 1. Our approach, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ABEX-RAT</span>, establishes a new state-of-the-art on the OSHA dataset, achieving the highest Weighted-F1 score of 92.82% and the highest macro-F1 score of 90.32%. This indicates that our framework demonstrates robust classification capability across both common and infrequent accident categories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">ABEX-RAT significantly outperforms both traditional fine-tuned models and zero-shot LLMs. For instance, our method surpasses the strong <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoBERTa-base</span> baseline by 2.0 percentage points in Weighted-F1 and 0.58 in macro-F1. In contrast, the zero-shot performance of capable LLMs like <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-8B</span> is substantially lower, underscoring the critical need for domain- and task-specific adaptation for this specialized classification task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, our framework also demonstrates a significant advantage over competitive SOTA baselines. It surpasses the directly fine-tuned <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-8B<sub id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub">SFT</sub></span> by a large margin (+18.23% in Weighted-F1 and +31.65% in macro-F1). This result validates our core hypothesis: the synergy of targeted data augmentation and robust training is a more effective and efficient strategy than brute-force fine-tuning of a large model. Furthermore, the considerable performance gain over the powerful <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-Embedding-8B</span> baseline, especially the jump of 18.68 percentage points in macro-F1, highlights the profound impact of our ABEX data augmentation and RAT training protocol. This validates that our approach not only improves overall accuracy but also substantially enhances the model’s ability to recognize underrepresented classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discussion</h2>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.02072v1/ab.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/125;" width="215" height="125" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Results of the ablation experiment.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Ablation Experiment</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To disentangle the contributions of the ABEX data augmentation and the RAT protocol, we conducted an ablation study comparing our full <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ABEX-RAT</span> model with two variants: one using only ABEX (<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">w/o RAT</span>) and another using only RAT on the original data (<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">w/o ABEX</span>). The results, shown in Figure 3, reveal a clear synergistic effect. The <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">w/o RAT</span> model achieves a strong macro-F1 score of 86.68%, demonstrating ABEX’s effectiveness in enriching the data. In contrast, the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">w/o ABEX</span> model yields a lower macro-F1 of 74.08%, confirming that RAT’s primary role is to enhance robustness. Our full <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ABEX-RAT</span> framework outperforms both, achieving the highest macro-F1 of 90.32%. This synergy is particularly evident in the recall, which jumps from 84.70% (<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">w/o RAT</span>) to 91.88% (<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ABEX-RAT</span>), suggesting that RAT significantly improves the model’s ability to identify challenging minority-class instances made more learnable by the augmented data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Fine-grained Performance Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.02072v1/cm_bold.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/195;" width="215" height="195" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Normalized confusion matrix.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further dissect the classification performance, we present the normalized confusion matrix in Figure 4. The matrix diagonal highlights the model’s robust recall across all categories, including underrepresented ones such as <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FIRES AND EXPLOSIONS</span> (91.30%), which provides strong evidence for the efficacy of our framework in mitigating class imbalance. The analysis also reveals that the most significant misclassification—25% of <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">TRANSPORTATION INCIDENTS</span> being predicted as <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CONTACT WITH OBJECTS AND EQUIPMENT</span>—is semantically plausible. Overall, the matrix offers a fine-grained validation of the model’s capabilities, substantiating the high macro-F1 score reported in Table 1 and confirming that the synergy of ABEX and RAT leads to a balanced and accurate performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we introduced ABEX-RAT, a novel and efficient framework for classifying occupational accident reports, specifically designed to address the challenges of data scarcity and class imbalance. Our approach synergizes a two-step abstractive-expansive data augmentation pipeline with a computationally efficient RAT protocol. Experimental results on the OSHA dataset demonstrate that ABEX-RAT establishes a new state-of-the-art, achieving a macro-F1 score of 90.32%. Our analyses confirm that this synergistic approach is superior to brute-force fine-tuning for specialized classification tasks. Future work will focus on adapting this framework to other tasks within the occupational safety domain, and exploring advanced methods to resolve semantic ambiguities between closely related classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
